 cdef:'The table lists settings by image type. The chart shows how contrast affects the result.',
 table:{cap:'Black and White Image to SVG Converter settings by image type',cols:['Image type','Threshold','Smoothness','Remove specks','Result'],rows:[['High-contrast poster','128','40%','20 px²','Bold shapes'],['Rubber stamp','140','30%','10 px²','Solid ink areas'],['Cutting stencil','128','25%','30 px²','Clean, connected shapes'],['Grayscale photo','110–140','50%','24 px²','High-contrast portrait']]},
 bars:{title:'Result quality by contrast',cap:'Guide, 1 to 5: higher contrast converts more cleanly.',rows:[['High contrast',5],['Medium contrast',4],['Low contrast',3],['Photograph',2]]},
 faq:[['Is the result really just one color?','Yes. The SVG contains a single black path on a transparent background, which suits cutting and stamping.'],['Can I use the SVG in a cutting machine?','Yes, single-path SVGs work in most cutting software. Check that islands and holes cut as you expect.'],['What threshold should I use?','128 is a good start. Lower it for more black, raise it for less, until the preview matches your intent.'],['What happens to a grayscale photo?','It becomes a high-contrast two-tone graphic. Midtones are forced to black or white at the threshold.'],['Is the background transparent?','Yes. White areas are not traced, so the SVG has no background box.']]
