C['photo-to-vector-converter']={
 def:'A photo to vector converter turns a photograph into stylised vector art made of stacked color layers. The result is a posterized illustration, not an exact copy of the photo.',
 how:[
  ['Read the photograph','The photo is decoded and resampled to a working size. A light Denoise reduces sensor grain.','Canvas · Denoise','pix'],
  ['Choose a large palette','Photos need 24 to 64 colors. A median-cut palette refined by k-means picks the most representative colors.','Median cut · k-means','pal'],
  ['Trace layer by layer','Stacked layers keep large soft regions together, and each is outlined at sub-pixel accuracy.','Stacked marching squares','wire'],
  ['Fit curves and render','Smooth Béziers turn the stair-step edges into flowing shapes, and the layers are written as a single SVG.','Bézier fit','vec']],
 why:['What to expect from a photo',[['Posterized look','Smooth gradients become color steps. More colors make the steps finer.'],['Larger files','Many layers and shapes can make the SVG bigger than the photo.'],['Style control','Fewer colors give a graphic pop-art look.']]],
 pdef:'Four steps convert a photo to vector art. The color count sets the style.',
 steps:[['Add a photo','Choose a photo with a clear subject and good contrast. Portraits and objects work well.','Busy scenes produce complex SVGs.'],['Pick the number of colors','Try 40 for a detailed result, or 6–10 for a bold pop-art style.','The size tile updates live.'],['Add light denoise','Set Denoise to 1 px to smooth grain.','Raise Remove specks if tiny shapes appear.'],['Download the vector art','Save the SVG for posters, prints and illustration.','Check the file size first.']],
 cdef:'The table lists settings by photo type. The chart shows how different photos convert.',
 table:{cap:'Photo to Vector Converter settings by photo type',cols:['Photo type','Colors','Detail','Smoothness','Remove specks','Result'],rows:[['Portrait','32–48','55%','60%','24 px²','Smooth poster portrait'],['Landscape','24–40','55%','55%','20 px²','Layered scenery'],['Product on plain background','16–32','65%','45%','16 px²','Clean product art'],['Pop-art style','6–10','60%','50%','20 px²','Bold graphic']]},
 bars:{title:'Vector fit by photo type',cap:'Guide, 1 to 5: simple subjects convert better.',rows:[['Pop-art portrait',4],['Product shot',3],['Portrait',3],['Busy landscape',2]]},
 faq:[['Can a photo become a perfect vector?','No. Photos contain smooth gradients and fine texture that vectors approximate with flat color layers, so the result looks like stylised artwork.'],['How many colors should I use?','Use 32–48 for detailed results and 6–10 for a bold, graphic style.'],['Why is the SVG larger than my photo?','A photo needs many shapes. Reduce Colors, raise Remove specks or increase Smoothness to shrink it.'],['Why do I see color bands?','Flat vector layers cannot hold a smooth gradient, so it appears as steps.'],['What is the result good for?','Posters, stickers, prints and illustration. It is not meant to replace the photo.']]
};

C['raster-to-vector-converter']={
 def:'A raster to vector converter rebuilds blurry or low-resolution pixel images as sharp, resolution-independent shapes. Raster images are grids of pixels; vectors are shapes defined by math.',
 how:[
  ['Read the raster pixels','Whatever the source, the image is decoded into a grid of RGBA pixels.','Canvas · RGBA','pix'],
  ['Upsample and smooth','Low-resolution images are enlarged with smoothing, and Denoise softens blocky pixels so curves can be recovered.','Upsample · Denoise','blur'],
  ['Reduce to a palette','A small palette of around ten colors separates the main areas of the image.','Median cut','pal'],
  ['Trace and render','Sub-pixel outlines and Bézier curves replace stair-steps with smooth, scalable edges.','Marching squares · Bézier fit','vec']],
 why:['Why convert raster to vector',[['Scales infinitely','A vector can be printed at any size without pixelation.'],['Blur is repaired','Smoothing and curve fitting turn blocky edges into clean ones.'],['Small files','Flat artwork is usually smaller as a vector.']]],
 pdef:'Four steps convert a raster image to a vector. Higher Smoothness helps blocky sources.',
 steps:[['Add your raster image','Drop any PNG, JPG, WebP, GIF or BMP. The larger the original, the better the result.','Very small images give rough edges.'],['Choose about ten colors','Match the number of main color areas in the picture.','Fewer colors give cleaner layers.'],['Raise smoothness','Set Smoothness near 60% and Denoise near 1.5 px for low-resolution sources.','Lower it for sharp-edged screenshots.'],['Download the vector','Save the SVG and scale it to any size.','Open it in a vector editor.']],
 cdef:'The table lists settings by raster type. The chart ranks how well each type converts.',
 table:{cap:'Raster to Vector Converter settings by raster type',cols:['Raster type','Colors','Detail','Smoothness','Denoise','Result'],rows:[['Low-resolution logo','4–8','75%','60%','1.5 px','Smooth shapes'],['Blurry icon','4–6','75%','60%','1.5 px','Recovered curves'],['Screenshot','8–12','80%','20%','0','Sharp edges'],['Photograph','32+','55%','60%','1 px','Posterized art']]},
 bars:{title:'Vector fit by raster type',cap:'Guide, 1 to 5.',rows:[['Low-res logo',4],['Blurry icon',4],['Screenshot',4],['Photograph',2]]},
 faq:[['What is the difference between raster and vector?','Raster images are grids of pixels that lose sharpness when enlarged. Vectors are shapes defined by math and stay sharp at any size.'],['Can it fix a blurry image?','It cannot add detail that was never there, but smoothing and curve fitting turn blocky edges into clean shapes.'],['Will the quality be perfect?','No. The vector is an approximation made of flat shapes. Flat artwork converts best.'],['Will the file be smaller?','For flat artwork usually yes. For photographs often no.'],['Does the result scale to any size?','Yes. That is the main benefit of vector.']]
};

module.exports=C;
